  plc=127.0.0.1:61054  gateway=127.0.0.1:61055  agent=127.0.0.1:61056

   N   throughput (ack/s)      latency median        slowest/fastest HMI    refusals
   1       1740 +/-  25%             488 us +/- 13%         1.00x              none
   2       3253 +/-  12%             498 us +/-  7%         1.03x              none
   5       3975 +/-   9%            1078 us +/- 10%         1.17x              0x0Ax4
  10       3783 +/-   9%            2165 us +/-  5%         1.28x              0x0Ax28

  N 1 -> 10: throughput 1740 -> 3783 ack/s, latency 488 -> 2165 us
  Peak throughput 3975 ack/s at N=5; within 10 % of it from N=5 on.
  So the path saturates at N=5 and does not improve to N=10: the gateway's
  state lock spans the PLC round trip and the agent serialises emission, so
  concurrent HMIs queue. Latency is then ~4x at N=10 against N=1 — queueing,
  not work. Capacity above this needs **distinct origins**, one enrolled identity
  per HMI, which the per-origin map and per-origin sequence (A.1) allow.

  32 write(s) refused or dropped across all N.
